  "notes": [
    "Arduino numbers map to ports as: D0-D7 = PD0-PD7, D8-D13 = PB0-PB5, A0-A5 = PC0-PC5. The chip pin order does not follow either sequence.",
    "A factory chip runs on the 8 MHz internal oscillator with CKDIV8 set, so it actually ticks at 1 MHz until you change the fuses. Arduino boards use an external 16 MHz crystal and fuses to match.",
    "VCC (7) and AVCC (20) must both be connected, with 100 nF at each. AVCC feeds the ADC and port C; leaving it floating gives you garbage analog readings and sometimes a chip that will not start.",
    "PB6/PB7 (9/10) are the crystal pins. On the internal oscillator they free up as two extra I/O, but the Arduino core does not expose them.",
    "RESET (1) needs a 10k pull-up to VCC. It is also the dW debug pin, and disabling it via fuse locks out ISP programming.",
    "20 mA per pin recommended, 40 mA absolute max, 200 mA total through VCC/GND.",
    "The ATmega328PB is a newer die with extra peripherals, but it is not a silent drop-in: some register names and vector numbers changed.",
    "The speed grade tracks the supply: 20 MHz needs at least 4.5 V, 10 MHz needs 2.7 V, and 4 MHz works down to 1.8 V. Running a 16 MHz crystal on 3.3 V is out of spec, which is why 3.3 V Pro Minis ship at 8 MHz."
  ],
